\section*{Problem Statement}
For any matrix $A$, the following statements are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.
    \item The minimal polynomial of $A$ has no repeated roots, meaning each linear factor has a power of 1.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The Extended Eigenspace and Jordan Form View}
When a matrix is non-defective, we can find a full set of $n$ eigenvector directions. This lets us split: 
\[ \mathbb{R}^n = E_{\lambda_1} \oplus E_{\lambda_2} \oplus \dots \oplus E_{\lambda_k} \]
where each $E_{\lambda_i} = \text{Ker}(A - \lambda_i I)$.

However, defective matrices collapse---their eigenspaces are too small and only form a subspace of $\mathbb{R}^n$. To get the missing dimensions, we expand $E_{\lambda_i}$ into the extended eigenspace, denoted $T_{\lambda_i}$.

Instead of stopping at $\text{Ker}(A-\lambda I)$, we look at vectors killed by higher powers of the operator:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Step 1: $\text{Ker}(A-\lambda I) \rightarrow$ the core eigenvectors
    \item Step 2: $\text{Ker}(A-\lambda I)^2 \rightarrow$ vectors that need 2 applications to drop to $\vec{0}$
    \item Step $m$: $\text{Ker}(A-\lambda I)^m \rightarrow$ the final boundary where the kernel stops growing
\end{itemize}

The kernel space of $(A-\lambda_i I)^k$ is called $T_{\lambda_i}$, aka the extended eigenspace. Every matrix has a Jordan Form---this basically means $T_{\lambda_i}$ has the same dimension as its algebraic multiplicity and thus all $T_{\lambda_i}$ decompose $\mathbb{R}^n$ just like $E_{\lambda_i}$:
\[ T_{\lambda_1} \oplus \dots \oplus T_{\lambda_k} = \mathbb{R}^n \]

\subsection*{Connecting the Onion Ring to Jordan Blocks and Chains}
To see how the Jordan Canonical Form arises naturally, we must connect the "onion ring" structure of $T_{\lambda_i}$ with individual Jordan blocks:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Extended Eigenspace ($T_{\lambda_i}$) = The Entire Onion Ring:} The full space $T_{\lambda_i}$ encompasses all nested kernel layers for eigenvalue $\lambda_i$. Its total dimension equals the algebraic multiplicity $m_i$.
    \item \textbf{1 Core Eigenvector = 1 Jordan Block:} The number of independent eigenvectors in the innermost core $\text{Ker}(A - \lambda_i I)$ equals the geometric multiplicity $g_i$. Each independent eigenvector serves as the terminus for \textbf{one individual Jordan chain}, which in turn yields \textbf{one Jordan block}.
    \item \textbf{1 Jordan Chain = 1 Path Inward:} Each chain originates in an outer layer $\text{Ker}(A-\lambda_i I)^k$ and steps radially inward toward an eigenvector in the core.
\end{itemize}

For a specific vector $\vec{x}_1 \in \text{Ker}(A-\lambda I)^3 \setminus \text{Ker}(A-\lambda I)^2$, repeatedly applying $(A-\lambda I)$ generates a chain of length 3 that terminates at an eigenvector in $\text{Ker}(A-\lambda I)$:
\[ \vec{x}_1 \xrightarrow{A-\lambda I} \vec{x}_2 \xrightarrow{A-\lambda I} \vec{x}_3 \xrightarrow{A-\lambda I} \mathbf{0} \]

In our example, $T_{\lambda_i}$ consists of three such chains:
\begin{align*}
    \vec{x}_1 &\rightarrow \vec{x}_2 \rightarrow \vec{x}_3 \\
    \vec{y}_1 &\rightarrow \vec{y}_2 \\
    \vec{z}_1 &
\end{align*}
Here, $T_{\lambda_i}$ has dimension 6, algebraic multiplicity 6, and geometric multiplicity 3 (corresponding to the 3 distinct chains and 3 Jordan blocks).

